sec.p:
Also, at least in hippocampal CA3 pyramidal neurons, it has been reported that local dendritic depolarizations are necessary for plasticity, and somatic spiking is not, consistent with the fact that local calcium levels, driven by local voltage, are the signals triggering plasticity (Brandalise et al., 2016). Similarly, in hippocampal CA1 neurons, local dendritic activity is sufficient for synaptic plasticity (Bittner et al., 2017).
(1)
Δw=η{(wmax−w)Δt,[Ca2+]NMDA>θLTPandDA↑−(w−wmin)Δt,[Ca2+]L−type>θLTDand DA↓0,basal DA level \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta w=\eta\left\{\begin{array}{cl} \left(w_{\max }-w\right) \Delta t, & {\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\mathrm{NMDA}}>\theta_{\mathrm{LTP}} \text { and } \mathrm{DA} \uparrow} \tag{1}\\ -\left(w-w_{\min }\right) \Delta t, & {\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{type}}>\theta_{\mathrm{LTD}} \text { and DA } \downarrow} \\ 0, \quad \text { basal DA level } \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Metaplasticity is also implemented in a simple way, given with Eq. 2. In most of the article, it only occurs if synaptic plasticity also occurs. During one update step, each threshold moves closer to the amplitude (maximum) of the calcium level evoked by an arriving pattern with a rate ηθ (θLTP follows the amplitude of [Ca]NMDA, and θLTD follows the amplitude of [Ca]L-type). This formulation of metaplasticity is very similar to the sliding threshold in the well-studied BCM learning rule, where the threshold follows a quantity derived from synaptic activity (Bienenstock et al., 1982). When a synapse is modified, both thresholds are updated.
(2)
ΔθLTP=ηθ([Ca2+]NMDAmax−θLTP)Δt,ΔθLTD=ηθ([Ca2+]L-typemax−θLTD)Δt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Delta \theta_{\text {LTP }} & =\eta_{\theta}\left(\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\text {NMDA }}^{\text {max }}-\theta_{\text {LTP }}\right) \Delta t, \\ \Delta \theta_{\text {LTD }} & =\eta_{\theta}\left(\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\text {L-type }}^{\text {max }}-\theta_{\text {LTD }}\right) \Delta t . \tag{2} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
When using supralinear integration of inputs, we have added an upper threshold for LTP, ΘLTP (Eq. 3). This threshold serves to stop synaptic strengthening once synapses are strong enough to evoke a plateau. With it, synapses are not strengthened to the maximal value, wmax, thus avoiding strong supralinear integration for the irrelevant patterns. (This is important for solving the NFBP, as will be seen below.) The upper threshold for LTP only plays a role in synaptic plasticity, and is not taken into account in metaplasticity. This means that metaplasticity is performed in the same way as in Eq. 2, and under the conditions in Eq. 1.
(3)
Δw=η{(wmax−w)Δt,θLTP<[Ca2+]NMDA<ΘLTPand DA↑−(w−wmin)Δt,[Ca2+]L−type>θLTDand DA↓0,basal DA level \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta w=\eta\left\{\begin{array}{l} \left(w_{\max }-w\right) \Delta t, \quad \theta_{\mathrm{LTP}}<\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\mathrm{NMDA}}<\Theta_{\mathrm{LTP}} \text { and DA } \uparrow \tag{3}\\ -\left(w-w_{\min }\right) \Delta t, \quad\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]_{\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{type}}>\theta_{\mathrm{LTD}} \text { and DA } \downarrow \\ 0, \quad \text { basal DA level } \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Such “thresholded” metaplasticity, which requires the same conditions to occur as synaptic plasticity, cannot solve reversal learning, a task used to demonstrate the plasticity–stability dilemma. Because of this, when simulating reversal learning, we relaxed the conditions for metaplasticity so that it can be triggered by any calcium level in activated synapses (i.e. no need for calcium to be above a threshold as in Eq. 1).

(4)
Δw=η{(wmax−w)Δt,θ<[Ca2+]<ΘLTPandDA↑−(w−wmin)Δt,[Ca2+]>θandDA↓0,basal DA level, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta w=\eta\left\{\begin{array}{cl} \left(w_{\max }-w\right) \Delta t, & \theta<\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]<\Theta_{\mathrm{LTP}} \text { and } \mathrm{DA} \uparrow \tag{4}\\ -\left(w-w_{\min }\right) \Delta t, & {\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]>\theta \text { and } \mathrm{DA} \downarrow} \\ 0, & \text { basal DA level } \end{array},\right.$$\end{document}
(5)
Δθ=ηθ([Ca2+]max−θ)Δt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta \theta=\eta_{\theta}\left(\left[\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\right]^{\max }-\theta\right) \Delta t, \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and we use both “thresholded” metaplasticity (θ is updated only when [Ca] goes above it, Fig. 12) and the relaxed condition for metaplasticity (θ is updated for any [Ca] level in active synapses, Figure 12–figure supplement 1). In both cases, the dynamics of the weights and thresholds are analogous to those when two separate calcium thresholds are used. As before, no metaplasticity (fixing the threshold to a low value) results in the synapses representing shared features not stabilizing (Figure 12–figure supplement 3). With one calcium threshold, in the case of the NFBP none of the synapses stabilize (as occurs when both thresholds are fixed in Fig. 8). Comparing Figure 12–figure supplement 3B3, B4 to Figs. 5B3, B4 and 6B3, B4 where just one of the two calcium thresholds is fixed, we see that having two separate thresholds allows for separate control over synaptic strengthening and weakening.

(6)
d[Ca]L-typedt=[Ca]∞−[Ca]L-typeτCa+k2Fd∑x∈{1.2,1.3}ICavx−pkt[Ca]L-type[Ca]L-type+Kd \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d[\mathrm{Ca}]_{\text {L-type }}}{d t}=\frac{[\mathrm{Ca}]_{\infty}-[\mathrm{Ca}]_{\text {L-type }}}{\tau_{\mathrm{Ca}}}+\frac{k}{2 F d} \sum_{x \in\{1.2,1.3\}} I_{\mathrm{Ca}_{v} x}-p k_{t} \frac{[\mathrm{Ca}]_{\text {L-type }}}{[\mathrm{Ca}]_{\text {L-type }}+K_{d}} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In this pool model we included calcium from both Cav1.2 and Cav1.3 channels, since there are no pharmacological agents that selectively block only one of the channels, i.e. the compounds used in the experiments which have determined Cav1.3 as necessary for LTD in cortico-striatal synapses would also block Cav1.2 channels in SPNs (Berger and Bartsch (2014); nimodipine in Wang et al. (2006) and Shindou et al. (2011); also, other non-selective L-type channel blockers are used to block LTD in the following studies: nifedipine in Calabresi et al. (1994); nitrendipine in Kreitzer and Malenka (2005); mibefradil in Fino et al. (2010); finally, experiments in Plotkin et al. (2013) have suggested that other VGCCs than Cav1.3 may be involved in activating calcium-induced calcium release, which is necessary for LTD).

The dual exponential synaptic model (a difference of two exponential functions) for a spike arriving at time 0 is represented by:
(7)
gsyn(t)=gmax1CτAτBτB−τA(exp⁡(−t−t0τB)−exp⁡(−t−t0τA)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{\text {syn }}(t)=g_{\max } \frac{1}{C} \frac{\tau_{A} \tau_{B}}{\tau_{B}-\tau_{A}}\left(\exp \left(-\frac{t-t_{0}}{\tau_{B}}\right)-\exp \left(-\frac{t-t_{0}}{\tau_{A}}\right)\right) \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where gmax is the maximal synaptic conductance, and τA and τB are the rise and decay time constants. C is a normalization factor given with:
C=τAτBτB−τA(exp⁡(−t∗τB)−exp⁡(−t∗τA))t⋆=τAτBτB−τAln⁡τBτA \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered}C=\frac{\tau_{A} \tau_{B}}{\tau_{B}-\tau_{A}}\left(\exp \left(-\frac{t^{*}}{\tau_{B}}\right)-\exp \left(-\frac{t^{*}}{\tau_{A}}\right)\right) \\ t^{\star}=\frac{\tau_{A} \tau_{B}}{\tau_{B}-\tau_{A}} \ln \frac{\tau_{B}}{\tau_{A}}\end{gathered}$$\end{document}
where t⋆ is the position of the maximum of the dual exponential function. This normalization ensures that the time-varying part of the dual exponential reaches a peak value of 1, which is then scaled by the maximal conductance gmax. (The implementation in NEURON contains a synaptic weight parameter, as well. Since in the dual exponential model we have fixed the weight to 1, it is omitted from Eq. 7.)

The synaptic current arising from the conductance gsyn is:
Isyn=gsyn(V−Erev) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle I_{\text {syn }}=g_{\text {syn }}\left(V-E_{\text {rev }}\right)$$\end{document}
Excitatory synapses have both AMPA and NMDA components, and NMDA synapses in addition have a Mg2+ block described by a sigmoidal function:
(8)
INMDA=gNMDA(V−Erev)σ;σ=11+ηexp⁡(−αV), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{NMDA}}=g_{\mathrm{NMDA}}\left(V-E_{\mathrm{rev}}\right) \sigma ; \sigma=\frac{1}{1+\eta \exp (-\alpha V)}, \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with parameters α = 0.062 and η = 0.38. The parameters of the dual exponential model for the glutamatergic AMPA and NMDA synapses, and the GABAergic synapses are given in Table 4.

The saturating synapse models are taken from Gao et al. (2021), which are a variation of the saturating synapse models in Destexhe et al. (1994). They are kinetic models which operate according to two different kinetic schemes depending on the presence or absence of neurotransmitters. When neurotransmitters arrive at the postsynaptic site, receptor dynamics are described by a kinetic scheme that models switching between closed (C) and open (O) states with rates of α and β:
C+T⇌αβO \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C+T \underset{\beta}{\stackrel{\alpha}{\rightleftharpoons}} O$$\end{document}
where [T] is the transmitter concentration. After neurotransmitter has been cleared from the synaptic cleft, receptor dynamics evolves according to a kinetic scheme that describes just the closing of the receptor with a rate β:
C←βO \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C \underset{\beta}{\leftarrow} O$$\end{document}
In this model, when a presynaptic spike arrives, neurotransmitter levels always reach a fixed saturating concentration in the synaptic cleft, [T]max, which lasts for a short time Tdur (a short glutamate lifetime in the synaptic cleft of cultured hippocampal synapses has been reported in Clements et al. (1992)). If another presynaptic spike arrives while the neurotransmitter pulse is still “on”, the pulse duration is lengthened by Tdur.

The synaptic conductance gsyn in this model is represented by the fraction of glutamate receptors in the open state, [O]:
gsyn=wgmax[O] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle g_{s y n}=w g_{\max }[O]$$\end{document}
where gmax is again the maximal synaptic conductance, and w is the synaptic weight which is modified by the learning rule. The parameters in the saturating synapse model are given in Table 5.
